**Theorem 22.9 (the exact inverse on the attained image).** Under the
separation condition (22.13), the augmented boundary record at a fixed
deterministic $t$ and the target

$$
\mathsf T_t=(\kappa,K_{[0,t]},\theta_{[0,t]},v_{[0,t]},D_{[0,t]})
\tag{22.16}
$$

are Borel inverses modulo the law-wise null sets of the paired source. The
inverse from boundary to target is

$$
\begin{aligned}
K_a&=U_a+\alpha_\kappa,\\
\theta_a&=\theta_0+\int_0^a
       \frac{A_\kappa(K_s)}{B_\kappa(K_s)}\,dV_s,\\
D_a&=D_0+\int_0^a
       \frac{\kappa b_\kappa(K_s)}{B_\kappa(K_s)}\,dV_s,
\qquad 0\leq a\leq t,
\end{aligned}
\tag{22.17}
$$

with the actual zero convention $D_a=D_0$. Define $\mathcal G_t$ to be
the set of target tuples in (22.16) with the prescribed initial phase
representatives, height in $I$ throughout, successful inherited variation
reconstruction returning $(|\kappa|,K-\alpha_\kappa)$, and convergent
integral versions of Lemma 22.4 reproducing the two phase paths in (22.17).
When its candidate set is double, require additionally
$q(\Xi_t)\ne q(-\Xi_t)$. At zero or on a singleton branch no such test is
required. These are Borel conditions on continuous path spaces: convergence
and the variation tests use countable limits, path identities can be tested
at rational times, and the remaining range and endpoint tests are Borel.
Under every fixed $\mathbf Q_\kappa$, $\mathcal G_t$ has full probability
by Lemma 22.4 and Theorem 22.6.

Let $F_t$ project a target tuple to its wrapped $v$ record and installed
endpoint reply, and set $\mathcal B_t=F_t(\mathcal G_t)$. This is the
precise attained image in the assertion. The decoder compares admissible
replies as in Theorem 22.6, then uses (22.17); assign a fixed default when
the variation, range, unique-match or convergence tests fail. On
$\mathcal G_t$ it is a left inverse of $F_t$, so $F_t$ is injective there.
The Borel injective-image theorem for standard Borel spaces makes
$\mathcal B_t$ Borel. Restricted to $\mathcal B_t$ the decoder is also a
right inverse. Both composites are pointwise identities on these sets.

**Proof.** Theorem 22.6 recovers the signed $\kappa$ and $U$. The first line
of (22.17) is the shifted-coordinate definition. Dividing the common-driver
equations in (22.2) by $B_\kappa$ gives the other lines, and Lemma 22.4
supplies their causal Borel versions. Substitution back into (22.2) reproduces
the same stochastic increments, so the forward endpoint reply is exactly the
observed reply. Conversely, projecting a reconstructed prefix and evaluating
the declared sensor returns its boundary record. The inverse is an identity
on the attained good image, not on arbitrary continuous records.

After acquisition, retained $\kappa$ and the current state determine the
conditional passive $Q$ transition law. Continued supplied $v$ observation
updates (22.17) on the same source and agrees with the known-calibration
inverse of L Theorem 55.2: both its inverse of $2B_\kappa(K)^2$ and
$U+\alpha_\kappa$ return the same actual height, and the phase integrals
then agree by (22.2). Restricting a decoded prefix to an earlier time returns
the actual earlier state. This is retrospective recovery; the later endpoint
reply was not available to the earlier observer. A conditional law is not a
prediction of future Brownian outcomes without future observations. No arbitrary intervention,
native control, or current-state-to-past inverse is supplied. $\square$


